= Six Gentlemen Decoction (Liù Jūnzǐ Tāng, 六君子湯)

_Four Gentlemen plus *陳皮* and *半夏*: the Spleen-qi tonic extended to handle the phlegm that Spleen vacuity generates. It is a derivative, so this entry does not restate the base — see four-gentlemen-decoction.typ for 四君子湯 and its 補中有瀉 architecture. Two things make it worth its own treatment. First, its structure is unusually clean: it is very nearly the *union of two base formulas*, 四君子湯 and 二陳湯, with 茯苓 and 甘草 serving in both — the Spleen tonic and the phlegm formula fused, which is the prescription form of the maxim 治痰先治脾, "to treat phlegm, first treat the Spleen." Second, and unusually for anything in this book: *as the Kampo formula Rikkunshitō it carries the best formula-level clinical evidence of any preparation treated here* — multiple randomized trials and a worked-out ghrelin-signalling mechanism. That evidence creates its own hazard, taken up under Misapplication: a formula with good biomedical trial data invites biomedical prescribing, and the trials enrolled patients by diagnosis rather than by pattern._

== Name & Meaning

*六君子湯* — *六* (six) + *君子* (gentleman) + *湯* (decoction), extending the name of its parent. The *君子* metaphor is the Confucian one unpacked in `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`: drugs mild and even-tempered (平和) enough to work by sustained support rather than force, with the secondary pun on *君* as the technical term for a formula's sovereign drug. Adding two more gentle drugs makes six.

*A counting quirk worth explaining, because it confuses readers of the ingredient list:* the formula is called "six" but *the pot usually holds eight*. 生薑 (fresh ginger) and 大棗 (jujube) are added in most versions and are *conventionally not counted* — the *薑棗* pair is a standard harmonizing-and-vehicle addition appended to a great many formulas, treated as part of the method rather than part of the prescription. *So "six gentlemen" counts the named drugs, not the contents of the decoction.*

*Standard English renderings:* "Six Gentlemen Decoction," "Six-Gentleman Decoction." *Japanese (Kampo):* 六君子湯 *Rikkunshitō* — and in the Japanese standardized-product system it is *TJ-43*, a designation that matters for reading the clinical literature (below). *Korean:* 육군자탕 *yukgunjatang*.

*A homonym trap, as with the parent:* *六君子* also names a famous political group — the *戊戌六君子*, the "Six Gentlemen" executed after the failure of the Hundred Days' Reform in 1898. Unrelated to the formula, which predates them by some four centuries, and sharing only the Confucian trope. Cf. the 四君子 painting-and-poetry quartet flagged in the parent entry.

== Source & History

*Attribution is genuinely variable and should be stated as such.* The formula is most commonly credited in modern texts to *虞摶 (Yu Tuan)'s 醫學正傳 (Yīxué Zhèngchuán,* "Orthodox Transmission of Medicine," 1515); it also appears in *薛己 (Xue Ji)'s* annotated sixteenth-century editions, and some lineages trace the combination earlier. *What is secure* is that it is a *Ming-period formalization* of a combination already implicit in Song practice — the Hejijufang had both parent formulas — rather than a single-author invention. *Flagged for verification against a specific edition* rather than asserted.

*Its position in the family.* The Four Gentlemen lineage grows by addition, each step answering a problem the previous one leaves open:
- *四君子湯* — tonifies Spleen qi. See `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *異功散* — *+ 陳皮*; adds qi-regulation so the supplementation does not stagnate.
- *六君子湯* — *+ 陳皮 + 半夏*; adds phlegm-transformation, because Spleen vacuity *generates* damp which congeals to phlegm.
- *香砂六君子湯* — *+ 木香 + 砂仁*; adds aromatic qi-moving for frank pain and distension.
- *參苓白朮散* branches off separately for damp with *chronic diarrhoea* — see `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ`.

*In modern practice* it is heavily used across China, Japan, Korea and Taiwan, and in Japan it is among the most prescribed Kampo preparations, routinely given alongside conventional treatment in gastroenterology and oncology.#footnote[Attribution to Yu Tuan's _Yīxué Zhèngchuán_ (醫學正傳, 1515) is the most common in modern formula texts, with variant attributions in the Xue Ji annotated editions and claims of earlier appearance; the formula is best understood as a Ming formalization of a Song-era combination. Flagged for verification against a specific edition rather than asserted. Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed., for the family chain and modern standing.]

== Composition

*Classical proportions.* Sources differ; the common modern rendering gives the four Gentlemen in roughly equal parts with 陳皮 and 半夏 added at comparable or slightly lower weight, plus the 薑棗 pair. *As with the parent formula, the equal-parts convention is a Song-and-Ming powder-dose habit rather than a claim that all six contribute equally*, and modern practice differentiates.

*Modern clinical decoction doses* (reference figures requiring verification against current pharmacopoeial and clinical practice): 人參 9–12 g (or 黨參 12–15 g); 白朮 9–12 g; 茯苓 9–12 g; 炙甘草 3–6 g; 陳皮 6–9 g; *半夏 (processed)* 6–9 g; 生薑 3–6 g; 大棗 3–5 pieces.

*Two processing requirements that are not optional:*
- *半夏 must be a processed form* — 清半夏 or 姜半夏. *生半夏 (raw) is toxic and is never given in decoction.* See `substances/ban-xia.typ`.
- *炙甘草* (honey-fried), not raw 生甘草, which is cooler and clears heat.

=== 人參, 白朮, 茯苓, 炙甘草 (君臣 — the Four Gentlemen core)

_Treated as one block, since its internal architecture is the subject of the parent entry and restating it here would duplicate. Each drug still carries its property line, per this book's self-contained rule._

- *人參 (rénshēn,* ginseng) — *君*: sweet, slightly bitter; slightly warm (微溫); Spleen, Lung, Heart, Kidney. *大補元氣*, *補脾益肺*, *生津*, *安神益智*. See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`.
- *白朮 (báizhú,* atractylodes rhizome) — *臣*: bitter, sweet; warm (溫); Spleen, Stomach. *健脾益氣*, *燥濕利水*, *止汗*, *安胎*. Usually *炒白朮*. *No substance entry yet.*
- *茯苓 (fúlíng,* poria) — *佐*: sweet, bland (淡); neutral (平); Spleen, Heart, Kidney. *利水滲濕*, *健脾*, *寧心安神*. See `substances/fuling.typ`.
- *炙甘草 (zhì gāncǎo,* honey-fried licorice) — *使*: sweet; warm (溫) in the honey-fried form; Heart, Lung, Spleen, Stomach. *補脾益氣*, *調和諸藥*, *緩急止痛*. See `substances/chinese-licorice.typ`.

*Function in this formula:* the tonify-and-drain core, unchanged — 人參 supplies qi, 白朮 strengthens the Spleen while drying damp, 茯苓 leaches damp out, 炙甘草 supplements and harmonizes. *What changes is its job description*: in the parent formula this quartet is the whole prescription; here it is the *half that addresses the cause*, while the two additions address the product.

=== 半夏 (bànxià, prepared pinellia) — 臣, minister

*Properties:* Acrid/pungent (辛); warm (溫); *有毒* (toxic — a formal property, hence the processing requirement). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺). *Principal actions:* *燥濕化痰* (dries damp and transforms phlegm — the category's chief drug), *降逆止嘔* (directs counterflow downward, stops vomiting), *消痞散結*.

*Function in this formula — and it supplies the two actions that define the extension.* First, *phlegm-transformation*: Spleen vacuity leaves food and fluid untransformed, the residue becomes damp, and damp congeals into *phlegm*; 半夏 is the drug that dries and dissolves it. Second, and clinically more conspicuous, *降逆止嘔* — the premier classical antiemetic action. *It is 半夏 that gives this formula its upward-directed symptom picture*: nausea, vomiting, and the copious white sputum that the parent formula does not address.

*Why this drug:* no other drug in common use combines strong phlegm-drying with strong antiemesis. *Which form matters:* 姜半夏 (ginger-and-alum processed) inclines toward the antiemetic action and is the apt choice where vomiting dominates; 清半夏 (alum-processed) inclines toward phlegm-damp. *生半夏 is never used here.*

See `substances/ban-xia.typ` for the five processed drug-forms, the raw drug's toxicity, and the 十八反 incompatibility.

=== 陳皮 (chénpí, aged tangerine peel) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Pungent (辛), bitter (苦), *aromatic* (芳香); warm (溫). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺). *Principal actions:* *理氣健脾* (regulates qi, strengthens the Spleen), *燥濕化痰* (dries damp, transforms phlegm).

*Function in this formula:* it does two jobs that answer two different problems. It *moves qi*, which keeps four sweet tonics from becoming cloying and obstructive — the function it already performs in *異功散*, the one-step-smaller member of the family. And it *assists 半夏 in transforming phlegm*, by a complementary route: where 半夏 dries and descends, 陳皮 moves and disperses, and phlegm that is being moved is easier to dissolve.

*The aged form is specified.* 陳皮 means "aged peel," and aged material (conventionally three years or more) is the standard, the harsher volatile fraction having mellowed. See `substances/tangerine-peel.typ`.

=== 生薑 (shēngjiāng, fresh ginger) — 佐使, assistant and envoy

*Properties:* Pungent (辛); warm (溫). *Channels:* Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃). *Principal actions:* *發散風寒*, *溫中止嘔* (warms the middle and stops vomiting — the canonical antiemetic, classically 嘔家聖藥, "the holy medicine of the vomiting patient"), *化痰止咳*, and *解半夏天南星毒* — *detoxifies pinellia and arisaema*.

*Function in this formula — and note that it serves three purposes at once.* It *reinforces the antiemetic action* alongside 半夏; it *warms the middle*, supporting the Spleen; and it is *半夏's classical corrective*, the drug the tradition pairs with pinellia precisely to moderate its toxicity. *The formula therefore contains both the toxic drug and its antidote*, which is not a coincidence but the standard classical pairing — the same logic by which 姜半夏 is processed with ginger juice in the first place.

See `substances/ginger.typ`.

=== 大棗 (dàzǎo, jujube) — 佐使, assistant and envoy

*Properties:* Sweet (甘) — intensely so; warm (溫). *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃); secondarily Heart (心), Lung (肺). *Principal actions:* *補中益氣* (supplements the centre and augments qi), *養血安神*, *緩和藥性* (moderates the harshness of other drugs).

*Function in this formula:* it supplements the Spleen alongside the sovereign and, with 生薑, forms the *薑棗* pair — the standard harmonizing couplet that moderates a formula and protects the Stomach. Its sweetness also *buffers the acrid-drying edge* of 半夏 and 陳皮, so that a formula containing two warm drying drugs does not desiccate a patient who is already depleted.

See `substances/jujube-dates.typ`.

== Formula Architecture

_For the tonify-and-drain core, see the parent entry. What follows is what the extension accomplishes, and why its structure is unusually tidy._

*The formula is very nearly the union of two base formulas.* Set out the memberships:
- *四君子湯* = 人參, 白朮, *茯苓*, *炙甘草*.
- *二陳湯* = 半夏, 陳皮, *茯苓*, *甘草*, 生薑, 烏梅 — the base formula of the entire damp-phlegm category (see `substances/ban-xia.typ`, `substances/tangerine-peel.typ`).
- *六君子湯* = 人參, 白朮, 茯苓, 炙甘草, 半夏, 陳皮 (+ 生薑, 大棗).

*茯苓 and 甘草 belong to both parents and serve in both capacities simultaneously* — 茯苓 drains damp for the phlegm formula and strengthens the Spleen for the tonic; 甘草 supplements and harmonizes for each. Only 烏梅 is left behind. *So 六君子湯 is, structurally, 四君子湯 ∪ (二陳湯 − 烏梅)*, with two shared members doing double duty — which is why six drugs accomplish what might be expected to need nine.

*And the fusion is the point, not an economy.* The maxim is *治痰先治脾* — "to treat phlegm, first treat the Spleen" — resting on the mechanism already set out in the parent entry: Spleen vacuity *generates* damp (*脾虛生濕*), and damp congeals to phlegm. Treating the phlegm alone (二陳湯 by itself) addresses the product while the factory keeps running; treating the Spleen alone (四君子湯 by itself) leaves phlegm already formed in place and, being sweet and cloying, may thicken it. *The combination treats cause and product at once, which is what a chronic phlegm-damp patient with an underlying vacuity actually needs.*

*The formula's direction is upward.* Its characteristic symptoms — nausea, vomiting, chest and epigastric oppression, copious sputum — are all *counterflow and accumulation above*, and 半夏's *降逆* action and 陳皮's dispersal are what address them. *This is the sharpest contrast with 參苓白朮散*, which takes the same base in the opposite direction, toward damp with chronic diarrhoea below. *Same vacuity, same damp, opposite manifestation, different elaboration* — and the two formulas together illustrate how one base formula is steered by what is added to it.

*What breaks if an ingredient is removed:*
- *Remove 半夏* and the formula reverts to 異功散: competent for vacuity with mild stagnation, useless for nausea and sputum.
- *Remove 陳皮* and the phlegm is dried but not moved, and the four tonics regain their cloying tendency.
- *Remove 生薑* and the formula loses both an antiemetic and 半夏's corrective.
- *Remove the Gentlemen core* and what remains is 二陳湯 — a phlegm formula with nothing supporting the Spleen that produced the phlegm.#footnote[The 四君子湯 / 二陳湯 union analysis, the 治痰先治脾 maxim, and the upward-versus-downward contrast with 參苓白朮散: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed., and standard formula-theory teaching texts; the set-membership framing is this book's own organization of standard material. 二陳湯 composition as given in `substances/ban-xia.typ`.]

== Indications & Pattern

*Pattern:* *脾胃氣虛兼痰濕* — Spleen-Stomach qi vacuity with phlegm-damp.

*Presenting signs:*
- *食少, 納呆* — poor appetite, no interest in food; early satiety.
- *噁心, 嘔吐* — nausea and vomiting; the sign that most reliably distinguishes this from the parent formula.
- *胸脘痞悶* — oppression and stuffiness of chest and epigastrium.
- *咳嗽痰多色白質稀* — cough with copious, white, thin sputum, easily expectorated.
- *大便溏* — loose stools; *倦怠乏力* — fatigue and weak limbs; *面色萎白* — a pale sallow complexion.
- *Tongue:* pale (舌淡) with a *white greasy coat* (苔白膩). *Pulse:* weak (虛), often slippery (滑) or soggy (濡).

*The diagnostic requirement is all three of: vacuity signs, a greasy coat, and an upward symptom (nausea, vomiting, or sputum).* Vacuity without the greasy coat is 四君子湯; the greasy coat without vacuity is 二陳湯 or 平胃散.

*Modern correlates — correlations, not equivalences:* functional dyspepsia, chronic gastritis, gastro-oesophageal reflux, chemotherapy-induced nausea and anorexia, post-operative ileus, anorexia of aging, early cancer cachexia, and chronic cough with copious sputum. *See the modern trap below, which for this formula is unusually important.*

*Differentiation:*
- *四君子湯* — vacuity without phlegm or nausea. See `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *異功散* — vacuity with mild fullness; one step smaller.
- *香砂六君子湯* — this formula plus aromatics, when frank *pain and distension* dominate.
- *參苓白朮散* — the same vacuity-plus-damp, directed *downward* to chronic diarrhoea. See `formulas/ginseng-poria-and-atractylodes-powder.typ`.
- *二陳湯* — phlegm-damp *without* vacuity: a robust patient, greasy coat, no fatigue or weak pulse.
- *溫膽湯* — phlegm with *heat*: yellow sticky sputum, yellow greasy coat, vexation, insomnia.
- *半夏厚朴湯* — phlegm with *qi constraint*: the globus sensation (梅核氣), wiry pulse, stress-linked.
- *理中丸* — frank Spleen-Stomach cold without phlegm.

== Misapplication & Look-Alike Patterns

_This formula is mild and its errors are rarely dramatic — but it contains two *warm, acrid, drying* drugs (半夏, 陳皮) on top of a sweet tonic base, and that combination defines what goes wrong. It dries the dry and warms the hot. Consequences below are clinical commonplaces except where flagged._

=== If given to the wrong pattern

- *Stomach yin vacuity (胃陰虛) — the characteristic error.* *Mechanism:* 半夏, 陳皮 and 白朮 are all drying, in a patient whose deficit is *fluid*, not qi. *Consequence:* worsened dry mouth and throat, *dry retching* rather than productive nausea, burning epigastric discomfort, a tongue that grows redder and barer. *Correction:* a moistening formula (益胃湯, 麥門冬湯 register). *A worthwhile nuance:* 麥門冬湯 itself contains a small amount of 半夏 within a largely moistening formula — so the drug is not absolutely forbidden in dryness, and the issue is *proportion*, not presence. See `substances/ban-xia.typ`.
- *Phlegm-heat (痰熱).* *Mechanism:* the formula is warm throughout; heat added to heat. *Consequence:* sputum turning yellow and sticky, bitter taste, vexation, worsening insomnia, a yellowing greasy coat. *Correction:* 溫膽湯 or the 清氣化痰丸 register.
- *Excess phlegm-damp without vacuity.* *Mechanism:* four sweet tonic drugs added to a middle burner that is full rather than empty. *Consequence:* increased fullness and oppression, appetite worsening rather than improving. *Correction:* 二陳湯 or 平胃散 alone; tonify later if vacuity emerges.
- *Food stagnation.* *Mechanism:* as above — supplementing a blocked middle. *Consequence:* worse distension, sour belching. *Correction:* 保和丸 first.
- *Acute exterior illness.* The standing rule against tonifying while a pathogen is unresolved — *閉門留寇*. *Consequence:* a protracted illness. *Correction:* release the exterior first.
- *A dispensing error rather than a pattern error, but it belongs here:* if a supplier provides *生半夏* (raw pinellia) in place of a processed form, the result is acute oropharyngeal burning and swelling on the first dose — not a slow failure but an immediate injury. *Always specify the processed form.* See `substances/ban-xia.typ`.

=== Patterns mistaken for this one

- *Phlegm-heat mistaken for phlegm-damp.* *Why it resembles:* both give nausea, oppression, copious sputum and a greasy coat. *Distinguishing sign:* *colour, in two places at once* — the sputum (white and thin versus yellow and sticky) and the tongue coat (white versus yellow). *What to give instead:* 溫膽湯. *Consequence of the error:* straightforward aggravation, as above.
- *Liver qi invading the Stomach (肝胃不和).* *Why it resembles:* nausea, belching, epigastric oppression and poor appetite — a close symptomatic match. *Distinguishing signs:* *rib-side* distension, symptoms that *fluctuate with mood and stress*, and a *wiry* (弦) pulse rather than a weak or slippery one. *What to give instead:* a Liver-coursing formula (半夏厚朴湯 where globus dominates; the 柴胡 register otherwise). *Consequence of the error:* tonifying constraint increases the distension.
- *Stomach yin vacuity.* *Why it resembles:* poor appetite with nausea and epigastric discomfort. *Distinguishing signs:* *hunger without desire to eat*, dry retching, a *red tongue with little or no coat* — the exact inverse of the greasy coat this formula requires. *What to give instead:* a moistening formula.
- *Food stagnation.* *Distinguishing signs:* *aversion* to food rather than reduced appetite, sour or rotten belching, discomfort *worse* with pressure, thick coat. *What to give instead:* 保和丸.
- *The modern trap — and this formula has a distinctive one, created by its own evidence base.* Rikkunshitō has genuine randomized-trial support in *functional dyspepsia* and *chemotherapy-induced anorexia*, which makes it tempting to prescribe *for the diagnosis*. But *the trials enrolled patients by biomedical diagnosis, not by Chinese-medical pattern* — and "functional dyspepsia" is a label covering several patterns, of which this formula fits one. Two consequences follow, and both cut against pattern-free use:
  - *The evidence does not license prescribing it to every dyspeptic patient.* A dyspepsia patient with phlegm-heat or Liver constraint falls inside the trial's enrolment criteria and outside the formula's indication.
  - *Some of the trials' heterogeneity is probably pattern heterogeneity* — a plausible reading of why effect sizes are modest, and an argument that pattern-based selection might *improve* the evidence rather than being an alternative to it. *Stated as this book's inference, not as a finding.*
  Separately, *the biomedical floor applies*: new-onset dyspepsia over 50, dysphagia, weight loss, gastrointestinal bleeding, persistent vomiting or anaemia are *alarm features* requiring investigation before any dyspepsia formula, classical or otherwise.#footnote[Misapplication consequences — the drying effect in yin vacuity, the warming effect in phlegm-heat, 閉門留寇 for tonifying an unresolved exterior — are clinical commonplaces: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. The 麥門冬湯 nuance (a small dose of 半夏 within a moistening formula) is standard formula-theory discussion and is noted in `substances/ban-xia.typ`. The enrolment-by-diagnosis critique of the rikkunshitō trial literature, and the suggestion that pattern heterogeneity may contribute to modest effect sizes, are this book's own inference and are flagged as such. Dyspepsia alarm features follow standard gastroenterology guidance.]

== Modifications & Derived Formulas

*Named derivatives:*
- *香砂六君子湯 (Xiāng Shā Liù Jūnzǐ Tāng)* — *+ 木香 + 砂仁*; adds aromatic qi-moving and middle-warming for frank epigastric and abdominal *pain and distension*. The most-used derivative, and the next step in the family chain.
- *柴芍六君子湯* — *+ 柴胡 + 白芍*; for Spleen vacuity with phlegm *and* Liver constraint, i.e. where the look-alike above is in fact a genuine dual pattern.
- *六君子湯加減* toward *七味白朮散* (with 藿香, 葛根) in paediatric practice.

*Common modifications (加減):*
- *+ 砂仁, 木香* — toward the 香砂 register, for pain and distension.
- *+ 黃連, 竹茹* — where mild heat complicates the phlegm (used cautiously; frank phlegm-heat contraindicates the base).
- *+ 蒼朮, 厚朴* — for heavier damp with a thicker coat.
- *+ 枳實* — for more stubborn fullness.
- *黨參 for 人參* — the routine modern substitution, milder and cheaper at larger dose.
- *姜半夏 rather than 清半夏* — where vomiting dominates; a choice of *drug-form*, not of dose.

*Relationship to the phlegm formulary:* removing the Gentlemen core returns *二陳湯*; adding heat-clearing drugs to 二陳湯 gives *溫膽湯*. The three formulas mark out the phlegm-damp territory between them — with vacuity, without vacuity, and with heat.

== Preparation & Administration

*Classical method.* As with its relatives, the Ming and Song sources give a coarse powder decocted by the dose rather than a large raw-herb brew; the modern practice is decoction. *The exact classical wording should be verified against a specific edition* rather than taken from this entry.

*Modern decoction:*
1. Combine at the doses above, using a *processed* 半夏 and *aged* 陳皮.
2. Soak in cold water 20–30 minutes; bring to a boil, then simmer; two successive decoctions, liquids combined.
3. *陳皮 is aromatic and its volatile fraction is driven off by prolonged hard boiling* — some practitioners add it in the later part of the decoction for that reason, though it is more commonly cooked with the rest.
4. Divide into *two doses daily*, taken *warm*.

*Timing — and here the formula differs from a plain tonic.* Tonics are classically taken before food; but where *nausea* is the leading complaint, *small frequent warm doses* are preferred, and taking the decoction *warm rather than hot or cold* matters, since both extremes provoke vomiting in a patient whose Stomach qi is already counterflowing. *This is practical rather than textual*, and is the usual clinical adjustment.

*Course.* Weeks; it is a chronic-condition formula and gentle enough for sustained use, with the licorice ceiling (below) the limiting factor.

*Modern prepared forms — and for this formula the standardization is historically consequential.* In Japan, rikkunshitō is manufactured as a *standardized granule extract with a fixed product designation (TJ-43)*, with controlled composition and dose. *That standardization is the precondition of the clinical trial literature discussed below*: fixed-composition products can be randomized and placebo-controlled in a way that individually-modified Chinese-style prescriptions cannot. It is also the trade-off — the formula gains testability and loses the 加減 flexibility that classical practice treats as essential. Chinese 六君子丸 and granules are likewise available; concentration ratios vary by manufacturer and are *not interchangeable gram-for-gram* with raw-herb decoction.#footnote[Preparation and the warm-and-frequent dosing adjustment for nausea: standard Chinese clinical practice. The Kampo standardized-extract system and the TJ product numbering, and its role in enabling randomized trials of fixed-composition formulas, are well documented in the Kampo literature; the observation that standardization trades 加減 flexibility for testability is this book's framing. Granule-to-decoction non-equivalence: manufacturer labelling.]

== Cautions & Contraindications

_Pattern-matching errors are treated above under Misapplication. This section covers pharmacological safety._

*Pattern contraindications (summary; mechanisms above):* Stomach yin vacuity and dryness; phlegm-heat; excess without vacuity; food stagnation; acute exterior illness.

*Ingredient-derived cautions:*
- *半夏 — the processing requirement is a safety matter, not a quality preference.* Only processed forms (清半夏, 姜半夏, 法半夏) are given internally; *生半夏 is toxic*, producing immediate oropharyngeal burning, numbness and swelling. Classically *半夏反烏頭* — pinellia counteracts aconite, among the *十八反* — *a canonical prohibition that is genuinely contested in modern practice*, as recorded in `substances/ban-xia.typ`.
- *半夏 and pregnancy — a real tension, not a simple rule.* Pinellia is traditionally cautioned in pregnancy, *yet* the *金匱要略* prescribes 小半夏加茯苓湯 for vomiting in pregnancy, and pinellia-containing formulas remain in use for hyperemesis in China and Japan. The corpus is internally divided; this is a practitioner's decision. See `substances/ban-xia.typ`.
- *炙甘草 / glycyrrhizin — the limiting factor on duration.* Sustained licorice intake produces *pseudoaldosteronism*: sodium and water retention, *hypokalaemia*, oedema, raised blood pressure. *Caution or avoid* in hypertension, oedema, hypokalaemia, heart failure and renal impairment; *interactions* with diuretics, corticosteroids and digoxin. See `substances/licorice-root.typ`.
- *人參* — reduced INR with *warfarin*; additive hypoglycaemia with *antidiabetic medication*; case reports with *MAO inhibitors*; caution in uncontrolled hypertension. Classically *人參反藜蘆* (十八反). See `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`.

*A Kampo-specific caution that does* not *apply here, stated to prevent a false alarm.* The interstitial-pneumonitis signal associated with Kampo preparations — notably with 小柴胡湯, and especially in combination with interferon in hepatitis patients — attaches principally to formulas containing *黃芩 (Scutellaria)*. *六君子湯 contains no 黃芩*, and the pneumonitis warning should not be transferred to it. Transferring a warning across formulas because both are Kampo is as much an error as transferring efficacy would be.

*Reference information, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[半夏 processing requirement, the raw drug's toxicity, 半夏反烏頭 and its contested status, and the pregnancy tension: see `substances/ban-xia.typ`. Glycyrrhizin pseudoaldosteronism: `substances/licorice-root.typ` and current herb-drug interaction literature. Ginseng interactions: `substances/korean-ginseng.typ`. The Kampo interstitial-pneumonitis signal is associated principally with 黃芩-containing formulas (notably 小柴胡湯, and with interferon co-administration) and is noted here specifically to mark that it does not apply to this composition — verify against current Japanese regulatory communications.]

== Modern Pharmacology

*This is the best-evidenced formula in this book, and the reason is structural as much as pharmacological.* As the Kampo preparation *rikkunshitō* it has a fixed composition, a fixed manufacturer and a product designation, which makes randomized placebo-controlled trials feasible in a way that individually-modified prescribing does not. *The evidence should be read as unusually good for a classical multi-herb formula, and still modest in absolute terms.*

*The ghrelin mechanism — worked out in more detail than for anything else here.* Rikkunshitō *enhances ghrelin signalling*, the orexigenic ("appetite-generating") gut-hormone pathway, by several reported routes: promoting ghrelin secretion, potentiating its action at the receptor, and inhibiting its degradation. Constituents implicated include *hesperidin* (from 陳皮), *atractylodin* (from 白朮) and *10-gingerol* (from 生薑), with *5-HT₂B/₂C receptor antagonism* proposed as a contributing mechanism for the secretion effect. *This is a coherent account linking named constituents, a defined receptor pathway, and the formula's classical indication — appetite loss with nausea — and such accounts are rare for compound formulas.*

*Clinical:*
- *Functional dyspepsia* — the best-represented indication, with Japanese randomized trials reporting symptomatic improvement, particularly in postprandial distress and early satiety. Effect sizes are *modest*; see the enrolment critique under Misapplication.
- *Chemotherapy-induced anorexia and nausea* — trials, largely in cisplatin-based regimens, report reduced anorexia and improved oral intake; used as supportive care alongside conventional antiemetics, not instead of them.
- *Gastro-oesophageal reflux* — some trial support as an adjunct to proton-pump inhibitors, particularly for PPI-refractory symptoms.
- *Anorexia of aging, early cancer cachexia, post-operative ileus* — smaller studies; preliminary.

*Caveats, stated plainly:* the literature is *overwhelmingly Japanese*, with limited independent replication elsewhere; many trials are small, and some are open-label; and publication bias in the complementary-medicine literature is well documented. *The ghrelin mechanism, though well developed, is largely preclinical* — demonstrating a plausible pathway is not the same as demonstrating that it accounts for the clinical effect.

*What has not been shown:* that the six-drug combination outperforms its components or a simpler Spleen tonic; and that pattern-matched prescribing outperforms diagnosis-matched prescribing, which is the question this formula's literature is uniquely positioned to answer and has not asked. *And the single-herb literature for 人參, 陳皮 or 半夏 is not evidence for the compound* — a distinction this book insists on and that reviews of rikkunshitō sometimes blur.#footnote[Rikkunshitō (TJ-43) randomized trials in functional dyspepsia, chemotherapy-induced anorexia and GERD: Japanese clinical literature from the 2000s onward — verify against current systematic reviews. The ghrelin-signalling mechanism, with hesperidin, atractylodin and 10-gingerol implicated and 5-HT₂B/₂C antagonism proposed, is developed in the Kampo pharmacology literature and is largely preclinical. The observation that standardization enabled the trial programme, and the question of pattern-matched versus diagnosis-matched prescribing, are this book's framing. Consistent with the summary already given in `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ` and the glossary's Rikkunshitō entry.]

